<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  macOS only lets an app hear a microphone if the bundle says why it wants one.
  Without this key the system does not show the permission dialog and does not
  list the app under Privacy & Security > Microphone: it silently refuses, and
  cpal hands back a stream of silence. That is the audio-input bug on macOS
  release builds (#59). `tauri dev` never showed it, because a dev run borrows
  the permission of the terminal or editor that launched it.

  Tauri merges this file into the bundle's generated Info.plist at build time,
  so it holds only what the generator does not already write.

  The sentence is the one the dialog puts after "Yames would like to access the
  microphone": what it is for, and where it goes.

  The matching entitlement, `com.apple.security.device.audio-input`, lives in
  `entitlements.plist`. A signed, notarized build runs with the hardened
  runtime, and the hardened runtime refuses the microphone without it, whatever
  this file says. Both are needed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>Yames listens to your instrument to score your timing and coach your practice. The sound is analysed on this Mac and is never uploaded.</string>
  </dict>
</plist>
